0.006200386, 0.03151527, -0.061725933, -0.023082938, -0.0023575916, -0.0037195694, 0.0173645, 0.025911456, 0.04168061, -0.003935261, 0.015932232, -5.8056715e-33, 0.060956024, -0.01579622, -0.008419511, 0.008253846, -0.048088208, 0.025284749, 0.0046258494, -0.024938019, 0.020089712, -0.054910675, -0.04907565, -0.0057653915, 0.036207914, 0.01082827, 0.047496855, -0.05066495, -0.009895767, -0.0154672675, -0.044673562, -0.057849973, -0.052528214, 0.0034965985, 0.081379846, 0.00700586, -0.0021774769, 0.035639245, 0.019773161, 0.014650211, -0.03873165, 0.036069434, 0.03824557, -0.0018202332, 0.0079959165, -0.07573561, 0.021390356, -0.0005511437, 0.0033450888, -0.018852953, 0.010615499, 0.010072964, 0.022214184, -0.0025553145, 0.0042282627, 0.03815452, 0.014547085, -0.020934101, -0.028304817, -0.03788459, -0.04026046, -0.0025792432, -0.041436378, 0.015191869, -0.020109441, 0.079388216, -0.009016362, -0.015084845, -0.0032309426, 0.040811487, -0.015223675, 0.01799348, -0.046579234, -0.016827138, 0.022076568, -0.0064482098, 0.023757493, 0.011179638, -0.070201986, 0.025151087, -0.030676266, -0.03994289, -0.0017974265, 0.012993651, -0.05687866, 0.047177333, 0.024033254, -0.02781268, -0.011250316, 0.011790781, 0.05095255, 0.059187766, -0.008546297, 0.02467106, 0.029782396, -0.027596159, 0.035061777, -0.005370627, -0.025977105, -0.025482658, 0.00057042687, 0.013988777, 0.032656055, 0.048299644, -0.0014211588, -0.020825783, -0.056720506, -0.012827576, 0.06529686, 0.010281512, 0.012519186, -0.045977157, 0.028666638, 0.03597663, 0.000121234596, -0.0025927245, 0.013157224, 0.0029036147, 0.0020116284, -0.02568572, 0.00082118425, 0.03052109, 0.014063082, -0.036486007, 0.037998144, -0.0049552587, -0.039560333, -0.006876656, 0.0010107723, -0.031565145, 0.034031834, 0.0068567125, -0.0456842, 0.02280904, -0.0070678927, -0.018153269, 0.004750587, 0.05820398, 0.05341702, 0.019375395, -0.030867208, -0.008465979, 0.02407192, 0.02234676, 2.927229e-07, 0.0046718894, -0.036187053, -0.03524071, -0.008056131, -0.040492035, 0.05567234, -0.04626887, -0.025437778, 0.048962887, -0.027038807, 0.10247339, -0.077609636, -0.021378553, 0.024473099, 0.01287414, 0.025641683, 0.038608406, -0.07274133, -0.052710503, -0.0063835434, 0.09373142, -0.013542086, -0.0033611923, -0.03690097, 0.014587252, 0.065670885, 0.010253799, -0.06641819, -0.0368842, -0.00037513068, 0.058947217, -0.09096279, 0.03301181, -0.031141963, -0.023134716, 0.021596566, -0.010771886, 0.049628064, 0.03886035, -0.052993927, 0.020682352, -0.008166754, 0.0059985192, -0.041886274, 0.011621266, 0.010506674, -0.03052051, -0.038317155, -0.024152437, -0.027941301, 0.0054527996, 0.04254476, -0.001821395, 0.070946865, -0.029605063, 0.022678288, 0.04476518, 0.035973065, 0.0273507, 0.081023715, -0.014523555, 0.012119574, 0.009897432, 0.029852575, 0.05604296, 0.01173195, -0.027774556, 2.568581e-34, -0.016191134, -0.09985034, 0.053966597, 0.034783687, -0.02669108, 0.04369123, 0.017883345, 0.015504967, -0.014375382, -0.008448083, -0.041458458], embedding_text: "Decision Case: Untitled Decision\nDecision ID: exec_001_test_DEC_001\nExecutive: exec_001_test\nCategory: pricing_strategy\nDate: 2024-03-15\n\nSituation:\nMegaCorp Japan, our largest customer with ¥25M annual contract value, submitted a renewal request with a demand for 20% discount. Their procurement team cited competitive offers from WorkMax Japan offering similar features at lower prices. Our Sales VP, Kenji Sato, strongly advocated for approving the full 20% discount, arguing that losing MegaCorp would be catastrophic for our quarterly numbers and would send negative signals to other strategic accounts. Our standard discount policy, managed by the CFO, allows maximum 15% discount for renewals regardless of customer size. The CFO, Raj Patel, expressed concern that approving 20% would set a dangerous precedent and impact our gross margins by approximately 4 percentage points. Customer Success team reported that MegaCorp has been satisfied with our product (NPS score of 72) but is under cost pressure from their parent company in the US. Contract expires in 45 days.\n\nDecision Made:\nApproved Option 3 with elements of Option 4: Offer 15% discount + ¥3M worth of premium features (normally ¥5M value: dedicated CSM, priority support, custom integrations, advanced analytics) structured as 3-year contract with discount reducing to 10% in year 3 as they realize value from premium features.\n\nRationale:\nThis decision balances multiple competing priorities: (1) Policy Compliance - 15% is within our established guidelines, maintaining consistency and fairness. (2) Value-Based Negotiation - Premium features cost us ¥800K to deliver but valued at ¥5M by customer, better economics than pure discount. (3) Long-term Alignment - Multi-year structure with graduating discount incentivizes mutual success. (4) Margin Protection - Effective discount ~17% vs 20% requested protects 2% gross margin (¥1.2M annually). (5) Precedent Setting - Shows we're flexible and creative but not pushovers. This approach honors our Customer Success First value (we're investing in their success with premium features) while maintaining Sustainable Growth (protecting margins) and Operational Excellence (following process). The CFO can defend this to the board as financially prudent, and Sales team can position it as a win.\n\nOutcome:\nSUCCESS - MegaCorp accepted the proposal within 1 week. Signed 3-year contract worth ¥72M total (vs ¥75M at full price, ¥60M at 20% discount). Customer appreciated the creative solution and felt valued beyond just price. The premium features (especially dedicated CSM and custom integrations) strengthened relationship significantly. CFO satisfied that margins protected and precedent is defensible. Sales team initially disappointed but came to see it as a better long-term win. Six months later, MegaCorp expanded by 500 seats due to strong relationship.\n\nLessons Learned:\nValue-adds are more powerful than pure discounts for strategic negotiations. They differentiate us from competitors who only compete on price. Multi-year deals with graduated discounts align incentives beautifully. Being creative beats being purely accommodating. The Sales VP was initially upset but became an advocate for this approach in future deals. This case is now our template for strategic account renewals.\n\nConfidence: 0.85\nPrecedent: No", category: 'pricing_negotiation', decision_made: 'Approved 15% discount + ¥3M value-adds, structured as 3-year deal', outcome: 'SUCCESS'});
CREATE (n11:Decision {date: neo4j.time.Date(2024, 2, 10), executive_id: 'exec_001_test', confidence: 0.78, embedding_model: 'sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2', executive_name: 'Akiko Tanaka', title: 'AI Features Investment - Phased Approach', financial_impact: 12000000.0, embedding_date: neo4j.time.DateTime(2025, 10, 24, 4, 45, 2, 959000000, tzinfo=<UTC>), rationale_summary: 'Reduce risk with phased approach. CTO convinced with strong business case. Balance innovation with financial discipline.', embedding_dimensions: 768, precedent: False, lessons_learned: "Start small, prove value, then scale. Don't bet the farm on unproven tech.", id: 'exec_001_test_DEC_002', embedding: [0.048293814, 0.08006148, -0.016443608, -0.06114381, -3.1919993e-05, -0.0155718755, -0.0036988514, 0.01763544, -0.03856327, -0.019190935, 0.024481248, 0.039451286, -0.037777208, 0.13957421, -0.044671547, 0.03343768, 0.013195779, 0.0092507005, 0.03338959, 0.0078945495, 0.003141755, -0.043486994, 0.0064958027, 0.011588567, -0.0009518762, 0.006207331, 0.013328344, -0.050693627, -0.028666621, -0.04981107, -0.020644214, 0.018183503, -0.007846879, -0.013165018, 2.1351484e-06, -0.072354205, -0.05508479, -0.0073112696, -0.024057785, 0.025009688, -0.0036000854, 0.06283754, -0.02069954, 0.030102942, -0.049804103, 0.043962322, 0.010387725, 0.07284713, -0.029898802, 0.029457122, -0.005682935, 0.031324245, -0.025890956, -0.008299222, -0.07828745, -0.063714705, -0.02383604, -0.0540391, 0.022584487, -0.045852, -0.0005436744, 0.054231457, 0.020724732, -0.04385762, 0.031223608, 0.062145684, 0.028057225, -0.055462454, -0.021905668, -0.0066988524, 0.043361742, 0.039653786, -0.011574054, -0.020784173, 0.019503979, -0.07763642, 0.027357135, -0.02297296, 0.008686817, 0.017838735, 0.018421233, 0.015893318, -0.035142023, 0.012869755, -0.02119548, 0.029977541, -0.03977837, -0.037715286, 0.044295292, 0.040024154, 0.051513676, -0.044786006, -0.0106880125, 0.020017797, -0.013761823, 0.026267853, 0.024980726, -0.009291