68575, -0.06558092, -0.024504794, -0.065209225, 0.010315412, 0.028971937, -0.0073795924, -0.0037866407, 0.0549318, 0.023306258, 0.06300902, -0.035078865, -0.005654754, -0.012260022, 0.025568753, -0.059035804, 0.07422191, -0.059668846, -0.026999814, -0.0017538703, 0.1777897, -0.01231462, -0.000133563, -0.046108846, -0.014865458, 0.12174629, 0.043617155, -0.03389949, 0.0030918615, 0.00416123, 0.022703769, -0.049446058, -0.016999854, -0.06365098, 0.0026028322, 0.061054252, 0.02050303, 0.0013318538, 0.031013422, -0.03343925, 0.018006353, 0.017676177, 0.03363228, 0.008381081, 0.03727663, -0.040217433, -0.064637244, 0.002916094, -0.09051048, 0.016914025, 0.007375907, 0.046335056, -0.03742842, 0.048831288, -0.019846968, 0.014638391, -0.035931807, -0.006901233, -0.049003426, 0.053258732, -0.0155424345, 0.017112646, -0.01805343, 0.027579669, 0.022317905, 0.016707638, 0.04185102, 2.3228434e-34, -0.029950842, -0.04769655, 0.0107529145, -0.00933696, 0.0003538921, 0.0112226, 0.009579825, 0.004325796, 0.006086271, -0.015096975, -0.0016030618], embedding_text: "Decision Case: Untitled Decision\nDecision ID: exec_001_test_DEC_003\nExecutive: exec_001_test\nCategory: personnel_crisis\nDate: 2024-06-10\n\nSituation:\nHiroshi Yamada, our VP of Engineering for 4 years and highly respected technical leader, gave notice. He received an offer from WorkMax Japan (our main competitor) for VP Engineering role with 40% salary increase (from ¥13M to ¥18M) plus significant equity grant. Hiroshi has been instrumental in building our engineering culture and technical architecture. His departure creates immediate concerns: 3 senior engineers mentioned they're reconsidering their positions, citing limited upward mobility. Our engineering org is 45 people, and Hiroshi directly manages 8 senior engineers. The team is in the middle of critical Q3 deliverables. Board members are pressuring me to counter-offer aggressively to retain him. Hiroshi has been transparent in our 1:1 conversations - he's not leaving primarily for money. He wants to be a CTO eventually, and we already have Yuki (co-founder) in that role with no plans to change. He feels he's learned what he can here and needs new challenges. I respect his honesty.\n\nDecision Made:\nOption 3 - Did not counter-offer financially. Instead, I had a candid 2-hour conversation with Hiroshi about his career goals. Acknowledged that we can't offer CTO role in reasonable timeframe (Yuki is 39, co-founder, excellent CTO with no plans to move). Blessed his decision to join WorkMax, framed it as right career move for him. Negotiated 8-week transition (vs standard 4) with specific knowledge transfer plan. Internally, promoted Yuki Tanaka (Senior Engineering Manager, 6 years with company) to VP Engineering with clear communication about growth path. Implemented company-wide career development program (¥5M investment): clear IC and management tracks, mentorship program, quarterly career conversations, external coaching for senior engineers. Used the ¥5M we would have spent on counter-offer to fund the career program instead.\n\nRationale:\nCounter-offering would have been reactive and short-sighted. Here's my thinking: (1) Root Cause - Hiroshi wants CTO role we can't genuinely offer. Money doesn't solve this. Matching salary would retain him 12-18 months then we'd be here again, having wasted ¥10M+. (2) Precedent - If we match external offers, we create 'get an offer to get a raise' culture. That's toxic. (3) Radical Transparency - I owe Hiroshi honesty about career constraints here. Better to support his growth elsewhere than string him along. (4) Internal Opportunity - Yuki (internal promotion) is ready and team trusts them. This creates succession pipeline. (5) Systemic Fix - Career development program addresses root cause for everyone, not just reactive to one person. This aligns with our Radical Transparency (honest about constraints), Sustainable Growth (don't create unsustainable comp precedent), and Operational Excellence (build system, not react). It's hard but right.\n\nOutcome:\nSUCCESS (Long-term) - Short-term was painful: Hiroshi left after excellent 8-week transition, 2 senior engineers also left in following 3 months (they were planning to anyway, Hiroshi departure was catalyst). Q3 deliverables delayed by 6 weeks. Board was nervous. However, 6 months later: (1) Yuki excelling as VP Engineering, brought fresh perspective and team energized. (2) Career development program reduced attrition from 12% to 6% annualized. (3) Senior engineers report much clearer growth paths. (4) We hired 2 exceptional senior engineers specifically because of career program. (5) Saved ¥5M annually vs counter-offer scenario. (6) WorkMax tried to poach 3 more of our engineers with offers, all declined citing career growth here. (7) Hiroshi and I remain friendly, he's now an informal advisor. Some competitors criticized us for 'letting talent go' but our retention actually improved. Team respected the transparency and authentic career development.\n\nLessons Learned:\nSometimes the right answer is letting people go to grow elsewhere. Money rarely solves career growth issues. Addressing systemic problems (career development) beats reactive compensation. Transparency about constraints builds more trust than false promises. Internal promotions can energize teams if person is ready. The ¥5M we 'saved' on counter-offer funded a program that benefited everyone. Short-term pain (2-3 months disruption) was worth long-term gain (better retention, stronger culture). Board initial concern turned to praise after 6-month results. This case is now part of our leadership training on handling departures.\n\nConfidence: 0.72\nPrecedent: No", category: 'people_retention', decision_made: 'Launched comprehensive career development program (¥5M annual investment)', outcome: 'SUCCESS'});
CREATE (n13:Company {arr: 4500000.0, size: 'Mid-Market', customer_since: '2023-03-18', name: 'Logistics Solutions KK', description: 'Supply chain management', industry: 'Logistics', id: 'company_logistics', type: 'customer', region: 'Japan', relationship: 'Active Customer'});
