637219, 0.08772087, -0.031536255, -0.022933956, 0.031014256, 0.0077354205, -0.04797196, 0.04134171, 0.02027425, -0.041761972, 0.009128165, 0.04919064, 0.0057148417, -0.011258322, -0.038132474, 0.01719909, 0.0030447734, -0.0021565836, -0.036528267, 0.023170376, -0.034230784, 0.054204684, -0.008342884, -0.03448279, -0.051071752, -0.0009004219, 0.021274518, 0.004039526, 0.012857918, 0.032244045, 0.02163483, 0.045488536, 0.015955437, 0.019647438, -0.060993142, 0.028852854, -0.039991353, -0.033810448, -0.0032808133, -0.024737012, 0.0025568092, 0.028158685, 0.03325288, -0.023869054, 0.019226702, -0.01111342, -0.02650394, 0.011948156, 0.014193554, 0.011902065, 0.08973174, 0.0070987404, 0.06101016, -0.013595275, 0.03865432, 0.049641863, 0.03234823, 0.031958293, 2.6878535e-34, -0.052272614, -0.089740045, 0.02946403, -0.008163511, -0.021563515, 0.0004087844, -0.040795933, -0.01899943, -0.0037870405, -0.01957873, -0.033138026], embedding_text: "Decision Case: Untitled Decision\nDecision ID: exec_001_test_DEC_004\nExecutive: exec_001_test\nCategory: market_expansion\nDate: 2024-07-22\n\nSituation:\nNexaTech has achieved strong position in Japan with 70% market share in our target segment (mid-size to enterprise B2B). However, our international presence is minimal - only 10% of revenue from outside Japan. Board is pushing hard for North America expansion, seeing it as necessary for next funding round and eventual IPO. Our VP Sales, backed by external consultants, presented comprehensive NA expansion plan: ¥200M investment over 18 months, dedicated US-based team of 25 people (sales, marketing, customer success), product localization (estimated ¥30M, 6 months for CTO team), legal entity setup, and aggressive go-to-market. Market analysis shows US B2B SaaS market is 10x larger than Japan, but also 5x more competitive. Our product would need English localization, USD pricing, US payment systems, and compliance (SOC2, GDPR). Current financial position: ¥500M cash, 24 months runway at current burn. NA expansion would increase burn by ¥11M/month, reducing runway to 18 months unless we fundraise. Board sentiment: 'This is the right time, market conditions favor expansion, we need growth story for investors.' CTO concerns: 'Product needs 6 months localization, team is at capacity, this will distract from Japan market where we're winning.' CFO analysis: 'Financially feasible but risky - we'd need to fundraise in 12 months.'\n\nDecision Made:\nOption 3 - Defer North America expansion for 12-18 months. Instead, invest the ¥200M to strengthen and expand Japan dominance with three initiatives: (1) Accelerate AI features launch to all products (¥80M) - this is what customers want. (2) Expand into mid-market segment below our current target - opportunity for 200+ new customers (¥70M in sales and marketing). (3) Product margin improvement and platform work to get to 70% gross margins (¥50M in engineering infrastructure). Communicated to board: 'We're at 70% of our segment in Japan but only 15% of total addressable market here. Massive opportunity to dominate Japan before spreading thin internationally. From position of strength (¥6B revenue, profitable, proven in Japan), NA expansion in 2025 will be much more successful than doing it now at ¥4.2B revenue with product that needs localization.'\n\nRationale:\nThis was a hard decision because board really wanted NA expansion. But here's my reasoning: (1) Market Opportunity - We have enormous room in Japan. Going from 70% of our segment to capturing more segments could double revenue (¥4.2B → ¥8B) in known market with proven playbook. (2) Product Readiness - Our product needs 6 months localization. Better to expand Japan with current product than rush into US with unready product. (3) Financial Prudence - ¥200M in Japan has 70% probability of success vs 50% in US based on our knowledge. Better ROI. (4) Team Focus - International expansion splits attention. Engineering distracted, sales confused, we risk losing focus in Japan where we're winning. (5) Competitive Position - Own Japan completely, then expand from strength. WorkMax (competitor) tried multi-market expansion early and struggled everywhere. (6) Fundraising Position - ¥6B revenue in Japan is stronger story than ¥4B stretched across markets. This aligns with Sustainable Growth (build sustainably) and Operational Excellence (do fewer things well). Board needed convincing with financial models showing Japan-first ROI is better. Yes, we may miss NA window, but better to dominate one market than be mediocre in two.\n\nOutcome:\nTOO EARLY TO FULLY ASSESS (decided July 2024, 3 months ago) - Early indicators positive: (1) Japan revenue up 28% since decision (Q3 vs Q2). (2) Successfully launched AI features to mid-market, acquired 32 new mid-market customers. (3) Team focus improved dramatically - engineering velocity up, sales team hitting targets. (4) Gross margins improved from 68% to 71% on path to 75% by Q1 2025. (5) Board initially disappointed but financial results convinced them. Two board members who pushed hardest for NA now say 'You were right to focus.' (6) Customer satisfaction in Japan at all-time high. (7) Competitors (WorkMax, EnterpriseFlow) are struggling with multi-market operations, validating our focused approach. (8) We're now in much stronger position for NA expansion in 2025 - better product, better margins, more cash, proven AI features. Plan to revisit NA in Q1 2025 from position of strength.\n\nLessons Learned:\nDominating one market beats being mediocre in many. Board pressure doesn't mean board is right - my job is to make right long-term decision even if unpopular short-term. Focus is a competitive advantage. Saying 'no' or 'not yet' to opportunities is strategic. Financial models showing Japan-first ROI convinced skeptical board members. We would have struggled in NA with current product and team capacity. Sometimes best growth strategy is going deeper not wider. This decision required conviction to push back on board - that's what CEO job is. In 3 more months we'll have clear validation of this approach.\n\nConfidence: 0.88\nPrecedent: No", category: 'policy_creation', decision_made: 'Created formal framework for strategic account discounts, based on DC_AKIKO_001', outcome: 'SUCCESS'});
CREATE (n15:Decision {date: neo4j.time.Date(2024, 6, 15), executive_id: 'exec_001_test', confidence: 0.75, embedding_model: 'sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2', executive_name: 'Akiko Tanaka', title: 'Board Decision - Defer Fundraising', financial_impact: 0.0, embedding_date: neo4j.time.DateTime(2025, 10, 24, 4, 45, 2, 986000000, tzinfo=<UTC>), rationale_summary: '18 months runway sufficient. Close to profitability. Better valuation if profitable or clear path visible.', embedding_dimensions: 768, precedent: False, lessons_learned: "Timing matters in fundraising. Raise when you don't need money.", id: 'exec_001_test_DEC_005', embedding: [0.013898646, 0.06946092, -0.031786755, -0.026548281, -0.015346853, 0.028875226, -0.062337518, 0.02095979, -0.043269794, -0.049416915, 0.049426746, 0.050985225, -0.03348587, 0.07314465, -0.025421739, 0.050756164, -0.015271589, 0.015172065, 0.019942064, -0.029635578, -0.0033043409, -0.013985845, 0.04039367, -0.0019042627, 0.016936576, -0.002043806, 0.0781141, -0.01307964, 0.001611485, 0.017219419, 0.00058380957, 0.006545792, 0.020960135, -0.068858154, 2.296917e-06, -0.035325576, -0.025705064, -0.005987976, -0.048035547, 0.04692543, -0.06961699, -0.05549118, -0.060188133, 0.028924908, -0.027451267, 0.05298684, -0.0118791945, 0.03762316, 0.012872255, 0.059465047, -0.021156065, 0.0076158694, -0.053231664, 0.0030149843, -0.0470065, -0.04161855, -0.0035837567, 0.018620444, -0.009715233, -0.062709756, 0.021182306, 0.023972712, -0.01706941, -0.044539705, -0.07488239, 0.015569208, -0.0010090356, -0.011223719, 0.02258628, 0.0087627405, 0.023206282, 0.019724976, 0.0015168231, 0.018874401, 0.019145325, -0.09660013, 0.059974037, 0.03294208, 0.013651921, -0.002639135, -0.037871342, 0.04775011, -0.019468583, -0.02257855, 0.0015902915, -0.034539487, -0.009431564, -0.014808334, -0.0058745313, 0.049585603, 0.044316433, -0.024341065, 0.023350162, 0.009886361, -0.008521956, 0.043189503, 0.073243335, 0.01976179, 0.030181028, -0.070127614, -0.038660