78032, 0.07310731, -0.019823315, -0.022768123, -0.01476495, 0.048446883, -0.04875287, 0.0056355633, -0.041190796, -0.02434531, -0.021265913, 0.051249605, 0.0012253849, 0.03586459, -0.042548094, -0.016634759, 0.0199933, 0.007709265, -0.03151881, -0.031632315, -0.010569285, 0.07966823, -0.02000828, -0.0045516226, 0.0045170495, -0.023973238, 0.043471035, 0.0025731337, 0.0041812155, 0.016304383, 0.009026732, 0.0073035327, -0.025167232, 0.00997613, 0.008767295, 0.03711839, 0.0055662533, -0.04913139, -0.025862712, -0.027468491, 0.02825247, 0.021365937, 0.051111523, -0.03813458, 0.032031797, -0.025886312, -0.021177782, -0.017602956, -0.007847605, -0.012525334, 0.06095683, 0.0052534314, 0.008756891, 0.00901749, 0.025893219, 0.03909395, -0.019797293, 0.004733468, 2.214026e-34, -0.010468587, -0.07690802, 0.059676178, -0.026359506, 0.013643156, 0.009913449, 0.011205232, 0.025245102, -0.003459013, 0.0037742797, -0.026688004], embedding_text: "Decision Case: Untitled Decision\nDecision ID: exec_002_test_DEC_001\nExecutive: exec_002_test\nCategory: budget_approval\nDate: 2024-02-10\n\nSituation:\nVP Sales, Kenji Sato, submitted Q1 budget revision requesting ¥30M increase (from ¥80M to ¥110M) to accelerate hiring of 10 Account Executives (AEs). His projection: additional ¥120M revenue in 12 months from new hires, arguing we're 'leaving money on the table' by not hiring faster. Sales team currently 15 AEs, quota ¥8M per AE annually. Current CAC payback: 14 months, which is healthy. Kenji's argument: 'We have 50+ qualified inbound leads we can't follow up on, competitors are winning deals because we're understaffed, market window is now.' Finance analysis showed: (1) Our best AEs (top 25% performers) achieve 120% of quota, average AEs hit 85%, bottom 25% hit only 60%. (2) New AE ramp time is 6 months to productivity. (3) At current win rates, 10 new AEs would generate ¥68M revenue (not ¥120M) based on 85% average performance. (4) Hiring 10 at once would mean mostly mid-level talent (not enough senior candidates available), likely pulling average performance down to 70%. (5) ¥30M investment with ¥68M revenue = 44% return in year 1, payback extended to 19 months (vs our 18-month hurdle rate). CEO was initially supportive of sales team growth but wanted my financial assessment. Board had approved annual budget with specific CAC payback requirements.\n\nDecision Made:\nApproved Option 3: ¥18M for 6 senior AEs (not 10), conditional on Q1 sales performance hitting 95% of team quota. Structure: Hire 3 AEs in Q1 immediately, evaluate after 60 days, then hire 3 more in Q2 if on track. Required VP Sales to provide weekly cohort performance data for new hires. Declined 4 additional hires that would have pushed payback beyond hurdle rate.\n\nRationale:\nThis decision is about financial discipline and risk management. Here's my analysis: (1) Math Doesn't Support 10 Hires - VP Sales projection of ¥120M revenue assumes all new hires perform at top 25% level. Historical data shows average performance is 85% of quota, and rapid hiring dilutes average to ~70%. Realistic projection is ¥68M, not ¥120M. At ¥30M cost for ¥68M revenue, ROI is 44% with 19-month payback, which fails our 18-month hurdle rate. This is optimism, not analysis. (2) Quality Over Quantity - 6 senior AEs will outperform 10 mixed-level AEs. We can be selective with 6 hires, get proven performers. Market for senior AE talent exists; market for 10 simultaneous hires means settling for mediocre. (3) Phased Approach Reduces Risk - Hiring 3, validating, then 3 more lets us test demand assumption. If first 3 succeed, we expand. If not, we saved ¥12M. This is option value. (4) Conditional = Accountability - Making it contingent on Q1 performance (95% of team quota) ensures sales team is actually capacity-constrained, not just under-performing with current team. If current team misses quota, adding more AEs won't help. (5) Budget Discipline - We approved annual budget with specific CAC payback requirements. Breaching our own hurdle rate 6 weeks into year sets bad precedent. This honors Financial Discipline (hurdle rate is not optional), Data-Driven Rigor (realistic projections beat optimistic ones), and Long-term Value Creation (¥68M at 44% ROI beats ¥0 at 100% ROI, but must be sustainable).\n\nOutcome:\nSUCCESS - Q1 performance exceeded expectations: existing team hit 102% of quota, validating capacity existed. Hired 3 senior AEs in Q1 as planned. After 60-day evaluation, their pipeline development was strong (leading indicators positive). Hired 3 more senior AEs in Q2. By Q4, the 6 AEs generated ¥52M in revenue (vs ¥48M projected for 6 AEs, above our expectations). CAC payback: 15.8 months (better than 16m projection). If we had hired 10 as originally requested, we would have spent ¥30M for estimated ¥68M (based on market for mid-level talent), payback 19 months - failing hurdle rate and creating margin pressure. VP Sales initially unhappy with 6 vs 10, but results converted him. He now uses cohort analysis proactively in all budget requests. CEO appreciated financial rigor. Board noted our discipline in exceeding hurdle rate requirements. This case established our framework for all growth investment decisions: realistic projections, hurdle rate compliance, phased approach with validation.\n\nLessons Learned:\nOptimistic revenue projections must be challenged with historical performance data. Hurdle rates exist for a reason - they're not negotiable based on opportunity excitement. Phased approach with validation beats big-bang hiring. Quality of hires matters more than quantity. Conditional approvals create accountability and option value. VP Sales learned to model conservatively - our working relationship improved significantly. Saying 'no' to full request but 'yes' to rational portion maintains both discipline and growth. This framework (realistic projections + hurdle rate + phased validation) is now standard for all investment decisions. The ¥12M we didn't spend on 4 additional hires was reallocated to product development with better ROI.\n\nConfidence: 0.88\nPrecedent: No", category: 'budget_allocation', decision_made: 'Approved 6 AEs (vs 10 requested), phased hiring with performance gates', outcome: 'PARTIAL_SUCCESS'});
CREATE (n17:Decision {date: neo4j.time.Date(2024, 2, 20), executive_id: 'exec_002_test', confidence: 0.9, embedding_model: 'sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2', executive_name: 'Raj Patel', title: 'Pricing Strategy - 10-15% Increase', financial_impact: 180000000.0, embedding_date: neo4j.time.DateTime(2025, 10, 24, 4, 45, 3, 6000000, tzinfo=<UTC>), rationale_summary: 'Product value increased 35% in 18 months but price unchanged. Market supports higher pricing. Minimal customer pushback.', embedding_dimensions: 768, precedent: False, lessons_learned: 'Price to value delivered. Test and learn. Customers respect transparency about value.', id: 'exec_002_test_DEC_002', embedding: [0.031333234, 0.028764205, -0.01891549, -0.031698477, -0.035981614, -0.010874401, -0.027940815, -0.0066310912, -0.13192026, -0.034755286, -0.031130131, 0.07029385, 0.0035107664, 0.10262904, -0.07036691, 0.004982423, -0.004571113, 0.055895895, 0.078248866, 0.007075471, 0.037807476, -0.036652427, 0.022614723, 0.012962348, 0.006238727, 0.012543902, -0.0037408823, -0.037287474, -0.0031367652, -0.03528139, 0.017155612, 0.005174869, 0.009162186, -0.0024549107, 2.0936563e-06, -0.05600601, -0.06951423, -0.019290496, -0.043061607, 0.030135116, 0.03069815, 0.019412536, -0.036212444, -0.013186808, -0.032938607, 0.024424447, 0.00801711, 0.105718926, 0.007192086, 0.035002258, -0.012655149, -0.050602295, -0.07715653, -0.005826883, -0.04761629, -0.057683334, -0.021678064, -0.057189763, -0.06663203, -0.021978624, -0.03134785, 0.0075737755, -0.008261836, -0.030286537, -0.030034, 0.031932097, -0.039981637, -0.010064904, -0.0043802597, -0.0008543864, 0.013324339, 0.017123848, 0.023587007, 0.00021347983, 0.014278252, -0.053229954, 0.00940396, -0.04160968, 0.044271726, 0.01825027, -0.019186268, 0.04509599, -0.0021609745, 0.017424127, -0.03399576, 0.019793143, -0.062301416, -0.059296947, 0.019545693, 0.024526715, -0.018947333, -0.03506558, -0.023020208, -0.008262651, -0.038852725, 0.025142604, 0.03629792, 0.0142