95, 0.052594017, 0.090262756, -0.037062254, -0.007857577, -0.027561605, 0.07436958, -0.073506154, 0.04056946, -0.038455773, 0.007512231, 0.00074543915, 0.0997415, 0.013948678, 0.017874965, -0.024331326, -0.0065027643, 0.031331595, 0.022793083, -0.02400371, 0.039076526, 0.0090054255, 0.08811565, 0.008212258, -0.069580674, 0.015386898, 0.004604032, -0.010652848, -0.00075498444, -0.023367627, 0.030233039, -0.00016285475, 0.043296445, 0.0050396826, 0.03792442, -0.011141755, 0.0058181654, -0.04680318, -0.0282795, 0.03571347, -0.08576708, -0.0051524234, 0.034051865, 0.048774157, -0.042331606, 0.027036434, -0.019952552, -0.005056875, 0.009082972, -0.014148688, 0.0047156387, 0.107793145, -0.029737532, -0.040161014, 0.029446587, 0.023046037, 0.05076214, 0.026643327, -0.02440856, 2.6460637e-34, -0.022807969, -0.061528575, 0.023964966, 0.009961893, 0.014648306, 0.020095248, -0.02849014, -0.046016157, -0.004344968, -0.017887566, -0.015930517], embedding_text: "Decision Case: Untitled Decision\nDecision ID: exec_003_test_DEC_003\nExecutive: exec_003_test\nCategory: team_management\nDate: 2024-05-10\n\nSituation:\nOur engineering team had grown from 30 to 45 engineers over 18 months (50% growth). I was still doing 1:1s with all 45 engineers directly - no middle management layer. This was unsustainable: (1) My calendar was 15-20 hours/week of 1:1s, leaving little time for architecture, strategy, or coding. (2) Some engineers were frustrated - getting only 30min every 2 weeks with me, not enough for career development. (3) New hires weren't getting onboarded well - I didn't have time to mentor everyone. (4) Operational details falling through cracks - I was bottleneck for many decisions. Several advisors and board members told me: 'You need to hire Engineering Managers. You can't scale without middle management. This is standard at 40+ engineers.' But I was resistant because: (1) I love working directly with engineers - don't want to be disconnected. (2) Engineering Managers often become 'people managers' not technical leaders - worried about that. (3) Adding management layer could slow down decision-making. (4) Cost: 3 EMs at ¥10M each = ¥30M annually. (5) Some of our best engineers are IC (individual contributor) track - don't want to manage people. However, the problems were real: I was stretched thin, engineers were getting less attention, and I wasn't doing strategic work CTO should do. One specific trigger: We missed a critical architecture review because I was in back-to-back 1:1s and couldn't attend. That failure cost us 2 weeks of rework. CEO Akiko raised concern: 'Yuki, you're working 60-hour weeks and still bottlenecked. Something needs to change.' She was right. I needed to decide: keep flat org and stay overwhelmed, or add management layer and risk losing engineering culture.\n\nDecision Made:\nOption 3 - Hybrid structure with clear technical leadership emphasis: (1) Promoted 2 internal senior engineers to Engineering Managers (Hiroshi for WorkFlow team, Kenji for TeamSync team). TalentHub stays reporting to me (smaller team). (2) Created Staff Engineer role (IC track) for technical leadership without people management. Promoted 2 engineers to Staff Engineer. (3) Structure: 2 EMs handle people management (1:1s, performance, hiring, career development) for their teams (~15 engineers each). Staff Engineers provide technical mentorship, architecture reviews, code quality standards across all teams. I (CTO) focus on: company-wide architecture, technical strategy, hiring senior talent, exec-level decisions. (4) Clear role definitions: EMs are technical (must code 30% of time) AND people managers. Not pure people managers. Staff Engineers are technical leaders on IC track - no people management but high influence. (5) Cost: ¥15M (two internal promotions, smaller raise than external hires). (6) Timeline: 1 month transition, I gradually hand off 1:1s to EMs while maintaining relationships.\n\nRationale:\nThis decision is about scaling without losing technical culture. Here's my thinking: (1) Can't Stay Flat Forever - At 45 engineers, I'm bottleneck. Will be worse at 60 engineers. Need to scale somehow. Flat org is beautiful but doesn't work at this size. (2) But Keep Technical Core - My fear about EMs is they become 'people managers' who don't code. That's how engineering culture dies. Solution: Require EMs to code 30% of time. If they can't keep up technically, they can't lead engineers. This is non-negotiable. (3) IC Track is Critical - Some of our best engineers (Sato, Tanaka) don't want to manage people but want to lead technically. Staff Engineer role gives them that path. They do architecture reviews, mentor engineers technically, set coding standards. This preserves technical excellence without forcing everyone into management. (4) Internal Promotions Cheaper and Cultural Fit - Hiroshi and Kenji have been here 4+ years, they know our culture, engineers trust them. External EM hire is ¥15M each + 3-month ramp + cultural risk. Internal promotion is ¥7.5M each and immediate impact. (5) Two EMs Not Three - I can still directly manage TalentHub team (smaller, 10 engineers). Don't need three EMs yet. This saves ¥15M and keeps me connected to engineering. (6) Gradual Transition - Handing off 30 engineers to EMs overnight would freak people out. Instead, 1-month transition where I introduce Hiroshi/Kenji in their new roles, we do joint 1:1s, then I gradually step back. (7) Clear Role Definitions Prevent Confusion - Wrote 2-page doc defining EM vs Staff Engineer roles, responsibilities, decision rights. This prevents 'who's in charge' conflicts. This honors Team Autonomy (EMs have real authority), Engineering Excellence (Staff Engineers maintain technical bar), and Sustainable Pace (frees me to do strategic work I'm drowning in). Yes, it's not pure flat org anymore, but 45 engineers reporting to one person is unsustainable. Better to add structure thoughtfully than burn out.\n\nOutcome:\nSUCCESS (with some bumps) - Six months later: (1) Hiroshi and Kenji doing great as EMs. Engineers trust them, they're handling people stuff well AND still coding 30%+ (their commits in GitHub prove it). (2) Staff Engineer role working better than expected - Sato and Tanaka love it. They're doing architecture reviews, technical mentoring, and have huge influence without managing people. This IC track is key retention tool. (3) My time allocation much better: 1:1s down to 5 hours/week (only TalentHub team + EMs + Staff Engineers), architecture/strategy up to 40%, coding up to 15%. I'm doing CTO work again, not just manager work. (4) Engineers initially nervous about 'hierarchy' but adapted quickly. Key was Hiroshi/Kenji are technical and respected. (5) One bump: First month was messy - confusion about who makes decisions, some engineers tried to go around EMs to me. Fixed by being clear: 'If it's people stuff, talk to your EM. If it's architecture, talk to me or Staff Engineers.' (6) Hiring improved dramatically - Hiroshi/Kenji now own hiring for their teams, we're hiring 2x faster than when I was bottleneck. (7) Onboarding better - new engineers get 1:1 with EM every week vs 30min every 2 weeks with me. (8) Cost ¥15M vs ¥30M if we'd hired external EMs - saved ¥15M. (9) Team velocity improved - engineers get unblocked faster, EMs handle operational stuff. (10) Cultural concern about hierarchy was overblown - engineers care that leaders are technical and care about them, not whether we're 'flat.' (11) Biggest win: We shipped AI features on time because I wasn't bottleneck. If I was still managing 45 engineers, I would've missed critical architecture decisions. (12) CEO Akiko: 'You seem less stressed and more strategic. Good decision.' (13) Planning to add 3rd EM when TalentHub team grows to 15+ engineers. (14) Staff Engineer role became recruiting advantage: 'We have IC track to Staff/Principal Engineer' - lots of senior engineers want this.\n\nLessons Learned:\nCTO can't scale 1:1 meetings forever - need management layer at 40+ engineers. But EMs must be technical (30% coding) or they lose credibility. Internal promotions beat external hires for EM roles (culture fit + cost). IC track (Staff Engineer) is critical for retaining senior technical talent who don't want to manage. Clear role definitions prevent organizational confusion. Gradual transition (1 month) beats overnight change. The fear of 'adding hierarchy' was bigger than reality - engineers care about technical leadership, not flat vs hierarchical. This decision freed me to do strategic CTO work instead of being in 20 1:1s/week. Best validation: Hiroshi (EM) said 'I can make bigger impact as EM than I could as senior engineer. Thanks for trusting me.' The hybrid model (EMs + Staff Engineers) preserves technical culture better than pure management model. Wish I'd done this 6 months earlier - would've saved me burnout.\n\nConfidence: 0.78\nPrecedent: No", category: 'process_improvement', decision_made: 'Redefined lead quality (MQL → SRL), implemented bi-directional SLAs', outcome: 'SUCCESS'});
CREATE (n30:Decision {date: neo4j.time.Date(2024, 7, 1), executive_id: 'exec_004_test', confidence: 0.82, embedding_model: 'sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2', executive_name: 'Sarah Kim', title: 'Content Quality Over Quantity', financial_impact: 8000000.0, embedding_date: neo4j.time.DateTime(2025, 10, 24, 4, 45, 3, 74000000, tzinfo=<UTC>), rationale_summary: "Surface-level content wasn't converting. Research reports drive 52% conversion vs 28% for blogs. Time on page +240%.", embedding_dimensions: 768, precedent: False, lessons_learned: 'One great piece beats 10 mediocre ones. Deep content wins in B2B.', id: 'exec_004_test_DEC_004', embedding: [0.018603267, 0.08063099, -0.011021624, -0.04174626, -0.00526561, -9.925501e-05, -0.07203105, 0.03876189, -0.055942714, -0.05432624, 0.034689654, 0.09603995, -0.017182676, 0.1157918, -0.040724896, 0.038921792, 0.016671, 0.024607254, 0.005970159, 0.00041044864, 0.018479936, -0.042727586, -0.0060057947, -0.01168429, 0.018890241, 0.0074273497, 0.046532482, -0.036955148, -0.006422624, -0.020877834, 0.016717682, 0.020958554, -0.026377251, -0.037691057, 2.229762e-06, -0.036536228, -0.037504524, -0.02033526, -0.015062586, 0.10485472, -0.046860557, 0.04575301, -0.024915477, 0.027470823, -0.04133981, 0.021732787, -0.02399281, 0.05913526, -0.012041785, 0.028219424, -0.0035349547, 0.041057594, -0.11073491, -0.01418161, -0.020923674, -0.053512122, -0.049720403, -0.0073354095, 0.03569642, -0.041279785, -0.05552698, 0.047535937, 0.009634946, -0.029755946, 0.020090083, 0.060398925, 0.06144259, 0.021072645, 0.0048180404, -0.029792873, 0.032991853, 0.014446611, -0.0021569617, 0.016791547, -0.005737447, -0.065008886, 0.06289716, -0.06578437, 0.016284816, -0.007907952, -0.048798323, 0.008854105, -0.024535421, -0.013766264, -0.018034292, -0.025425928, -0.052847397, -0.037952602, 0.024945924, 0.04108701, 0.025605056, -0.023077948, 0.0029235657, 0.004335617, 0.0035853353, 0.03448355, 0.07626546, -0.018157773, 0.0085227825, -0.043028943, -0.0